1
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:06,660
Thank you.

2
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,618
Thank you, everybody. All right, everybody, go ahead and have a seat.

3
00:00:11,419 --> 00:00:13,038
How's everybody doing today?

4
00:00:17,018 --> 00:00:18,859
How about Tim Spicer?

5
00:00:24,518 --> 00:00:30,199
I am here with students at Wakefield High School in Arlington, Virginia.

6
00:00:31,018 --> 00:00:36,158
And we've got students tuning in from all across America from kindergarten through twelfth grade.

7
00:00:36,158 --> 00:00:37,529
And I am just so glad that all could join us today.

8
00:00:37,529 --> 00:00:40,270
so glad that all could join us today.

9
00:00:40,270 --> 00:00:44,381
And I want to thank Wakefield for being such an outstanding host.

10
00:00:44,381 --> 00:00:46,780
Give yourselves a big round of applause.

11
00:00:52,719 --> 00:00:57,759
Now I know that for many of you, today is the first day of school.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:08,099
And for those of you in kindergarten or starting middle or high school, it's your first day in a new school. So it's understandable if you're a little nervous.

13
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:12,700
I imagine there are some seniors out there who are feeling pretty good right now.

14
00:01:12,959 --> 00:01:14,980
With just one more year to go.

15
00:01:17,140 --> 00:01:25,218
And no matter what grade you're in, some of you are probably wishing it we're still summer, and you could have stayed in bed just a little bit longer this morning.

16
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:27,879
I know that feel.

17
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:57,060
When I was Young, my family lived overseas. I lived in Indonesia for a few years, and my mother, she didn't have the money to send me where all the American kids went to school, but she thought it was important for me to keep up with an American education. So she decided to teach me extra lessons herself Monday through Friday. But because she had to go to work, the only time she could do it was at 4:30 in the morning.

18
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:02,218
Now as you might imagine, I wasn't too happy about getting up that early.

19
00:02:02,459 --> 00:02:05,799
And a lot of times I'd fall asleep right there at the kitchen table.

20
00:02:06,578 --> 00:02:13,478
But whenever I'd complain, my mother would just give me one of those looks and shesay. This is no picnic for me either, Buster.

21
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:23,658
So I know that some of you are still adjusting to being back at school, but I'm here today because I have something important to discuss with you.

22
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:32,000
I'm here because I want to talk with you about your education and what's expected of all of you in this new school year.

23
00:02:32,759 --> 00:02:38,960
I've given a lot of speeches about education, and I've talked about responsibility a lot.

24
00:02:39,199 --> 00:02:45,598
I've talked about teachers responsibility for inspiring students and pushing you to learn.

25
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:56,800
I've talked about your parents responsibility for making sure you stay on track and you get your homework done and don't spend every waking hour in front of the tv or with the Xbox.

26
00:02:57,400 --> 00:02:59,418
I've talked a lot about.

27
00:02:59,658 --> 00:03:08,020
Your government's responsibility for setting high standards and supporting teachers and principals and turning around schools that aren't working.

28
00:03:08,259 --> 00:03:11,680
Where students aren't getting the opportunities that they deserve.

29
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:14,060
But at the end of the day.

30
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:24,860
We can have the most dedicated teachers, the most supportive parents, the best schools in the world, and none of it will make a difference. None of it will matter.

31
00:03:25,098 --> 00:03:28,919
Unless all of you fulfill your responsibilities.

32
00:03:29,538 --> 00:03:41,900
Unless you show up to those schools, unless you pay attention to those teachers, unless you listen to your parents and grandparents and other adults and put in the hard work it takes to succeed.

33
00:03:42,938 --> 00:03:45,158
That's what I want to focus on today.

34
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:49,360
The responsibility each of you has for your education.

35
00:03:50,038 --> 00:03:53,319
I want to start with the responsibility you have to yourself.

36
00:03:53,978 --> 00:03:57,598
Every single one of you has something that you're good at.

37
00:03:58,079 --> 00:04:00,938
Every single one of you has something to offer.

38
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:06,000
And you have a responsibility to yourself to discover what that is.

39
00:04:06,639 --> 00:04:10,000
That's the opportunity an education can provide.

40
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:21,079
Maybe you could be a great writer, maybe even good enough to write a book or articles in a newspaper, but you might not know it until you write that English paper.

41
00:04:21,399 --> 00:04:24,278
That English class paper that'S Assigned to you.

42
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:37,040
Maybe you could be an innovator or an inventor, maybe even good enough to come up with the next iphone or the new medicine or vaccine, but you might not know it until you do your project for your science class.

43
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:43,159
Maybe you could be a mayor or a Senator or a Supreme Court justice.

44
00:04:43,639 --> 00:04:48,560
But you might not know that until you join student government or the debate team.

45
00:04:49,879 --> 00:04:55,120
And no matter what you want to do with your life, I guarantee that you'll need an education to do it.

46
00:04:55,579 --> 00:04:59,079
You want to be a doctor or a teacher or a police officer.

47
00:04:59,319 --> 00:05:08,079
You want to be a nurse or an architect, a lawyer or a member of our military, you're going to need a good education for every single one of those careers.

48
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:12,759
You cannot drop out of school and just drop into a good job.

49
00:05:13,459 --> 00:05:18,139
You've got to train for it and work for it and learn for it.

50
00:05:18,838 --> 00:05:21,959
And this isn't just important for your own life and your own future.

51
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:28,019
What you make of your education will decide nothing less than the future of this country.

52
00:05:28,259 --> 00:05:31,480
The future of America depends on you.

53
00:05:32,519 --> 00:05:39,600
What you're learning in school today will determine whether we as a nation can meet our greatest challenges in the future.

54
00:05:40,579 --> 00:05:50,778
You'll need the knowledge and problem solving skills you learn in science and math to cure diseases like cancer and aids, and to develop new energy technologies and protect our environment.

55
00:05:51,199 --> 00:06:02,160
You'll need the insights and critical thinking skills you gain in history and social studies to fight poverty and homelessness, crime and discrimination, and make our nation more fair and more free.

56
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:10,980
You'll need the creativity and ingenuity you develop in all your classes to build new companies that create new jobs and boost our economy.

57
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:21,319
We need every single one of you to develop your talents and your skills and your intellect, so you can help us old folks solve our most difficult problems.

58
00:06:22,379 --> 00:06:23,920
If you don't do that.

59
00:06:24,319 --> 00:06:29,338
If you quit on school, you're not just quitting on yourself, you're quitting on your country.

60
00:06:30,620 --> 00:06:33,319
Now I know it's not always easy to do well in school.

61
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:37,319
I know a lot of you have challenges in your lives right now.

62
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:39,939
They can make it hard to focus on your schoolwork.

63
00:06:40,879 --> 00:06:42,920
I get it. I know what it's like.

64
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:46,399
My father left my family when I was two years old.

65
00:06:46,639 --> 00:06:54,980
Nye was raised by a single mom who had to work and who struggled at times to pay the bills and wasn't always able to give us the things that other kids had.

66
00:06:55,620 --> 00:06:58,800
There were times when I missed having a father in my life.

67
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:02,579
There were times when I was lonely and I felt like I didn't fit in.

68
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:10,579
So I wasn't always as focused as I should have been on school, and I did some things that I'm not proud of, and I got in more trouble than I should have.

69
00:07:11,019 --> 00:07:12,278
And my life.

70
00:07:12,639 --> 00:07:15,740
Could have easily taken a turn for the worst.

71
00:07:16,639 --> 00:07:18,459
But I was lucky.

72
00:07:19,079 --> 00:07:24,680
I got a lot of second chances, and I had the opportunity to go to college and law school and follow my dreams.

73
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:34,300
My wife, our first lady, Michelle Obama, she has a similar story. Neither of her parents had gone to college, and they didn't have a lot of money.

74
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:39,399
But they worked hard, and she worked hard so that she could go to the best schools in this country.

75
00:07:40,740 --> 00:07:47,639
Some of you might not have those advantages. Maybe you don't have adults in your life who give you the support that you need.

76
00:07:48,199 --> 00:07:52,199
Maybe someone in your family has lost their job and there's not enough money to go.

77
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:55,778
Maybe you live in a neighborhood where you don't feel safe.

78
00:07:56,019 --> 00:08:00,560
Or have friends who are pressuring you to do things you know aren't right.

79
00:08:01,259 --> 00:08:02,920
But at the end of the day.

80
00:08:03,379 --> 00:08:11,439
The circumstances of your life, what you look like, where you come from, how much money you have, what you've got going on at home.

81
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:16,860
None of that is an excuse for neglecting your homework or having a bad attitude in school.

82
00:08:17,420 --> 00:08:24,660
That's no excuse for talking back to your teacher or cutting class or dropping out of school. There is no excuse.

83
00:08:24,899 --> 00:08:26,040
For not trying.

84
00:08:27,519 --> 00:08:31,199
Where you are right now doesn't have to determine where you'll end up.

85
00:08:31,579 --> 00:08:38,620
No one's written your destiny for you, because here in America, you write your own destiny, you make your own future.

86
00:08:39,658 --> 00:08:44,178
That's what Young people like you are doing every day all across America.

87
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:48,840
Young people like Jasmine Perez from Roma, Texas.

88
00:08:49,080 --> 00:09:06,580
Yasmin didn't speak English when she first started school. Neither of her parents had gone to college, but she worked hard, earned good grades and got a scholarship to Brown University, as now in graduate school, studying public health on her way to becoming doctor Jasmine Perez.

89
00:09:07,558 --> 00:09:15,960
I'm thinking about Andoni Schultz from Los Altos, California, who's fought brain cancer since he was three.

90
00:09:16,778 --> 00:09:26,399
He's had endure all sorts of treatments and surgeries, one of which affected his memory. So it took him much longer, hundreds of extra hours to do his schoolwork.

91
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:29,320
But he never fell behind.

92
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:31,620
He's headed to college this fall.

93
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:36,580
And then there's seatel Steve from my hometown of Chicago, Illinois.

94
00:09:37,178 --> 00:09:52,840
Even when bouncing from Foster home to Foster home in the toughest neighborhoods in the city, she managed to get a job at a local health care center, start a program to keep Young people out of gangs, and she's on track to graduate high school with honors and go on to college.

95
00:09:53,980 --> 00:09:55,038
A Jasmine.

96
00:09:55,379 --> 00:10:00,120
And Doney and Chantel aren't any different from any of you.

97
00:10:00,678 --> 00:10:03,678
They face challenges in their lives just like you do.

98
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:08,178
In some cases, they've got it a lot worse off than many of you.

99
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:11,038
But they refused to give up.

100
00:10:11,418 --> 00:10:19,740
They chose to take responsibility for their lives, for their education and set goals for themselves. And I expect all of you to do the same.

101
00:10:20,860 --> 00:10:27,080
That's why today, I'm calling on each of you to set your own goals for your education and do everything you can to meet them.

102
00:10:28,058 --> 00:10:31,759
Your goal can be something as simple as doing all your homework.

103
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:37,798
Paying attention in class or spending some time each day reading a book.

104
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:43,720
Maybe you'll decide to get involved in an extracurricular activity or volunteer in your community.

105
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:51,538
Maybe you'll decide to stand up for kids who are being teased or bullied because of who they are or how they look.

106
00:10:51,940 --> 00:10:58,120
Because you believe, like I do, that all Young people deserve a safe environment to study and learn.

107
00:10:59,340 --> 00:11:14,200
Maybe you'll decide to take better care of yourself so you can be more ready to learn. And along those lines, by the way, I hope all of you are washing your hands a lot and that you stay home from school when you don't feel well, so we can keep people from getting the flu this fall and winter.

108
00:11:15,038 --> 00:11:18,960
But whatever you resolve to do, I want you to commit to it.

109
00:11:19,418 --> 00:11:21,259
I want you to really work at it.

110
00:11:22,158 --> 00:11:34,620
I know that sometimes you get that sense from tv that you can be rich and successful without any hard work, that your ticket to success is through rapping or basketball or being a reality tv star.

111
00:11:35,820 --> 00:11:38,298
Chances are you're not going to be any of those things.

112
00:11:39,639 --> 00:11:43,340
The truth is, being successful is hard.

113
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:47,759
You won't love every subject that you study.

114
00:11:48,658 --> 00:11:52,360
You won't click with every teacher that you have.

115
00:11:53,200 --> 00:12:00,080
Not every homework assignment will seem completely relevant to your life right at this minute.

116
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:05,658
And you won't necessarily succeed in everything the first time you try.

117
00:12:06,278 --> 00:12:07,600
That's okay.

118
00:12:08,379 --> 00:12:12,620
Some of the most successful people in the world are the ones whohad the most failures.

119
00:12:14,798 --> 00:12:16,259
Jk Rawlings?

120
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:19,139
Who wrote Harry Potter?

121
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:25,158
Her first Harry Potter book was rejected twelve times before it was finally published.

122
00:12:26,558 --> 00:12:29,860
Michael Jordan was cut from his high school basketball team.

123
00:12:30,918 --> 00:12:42,298
He lost hundreds of games and missed thousands of shots during his career, but he once said, I have failed over and over and over again in my life, and that's why I succeed.

124
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:53,340
These people succeeded because they understood that you can't let your failures define you, you have to let your failures teach you.

125
00:12:53,658 --> 00:12:57,259
You have to let them show you what to do differently the next time.

126
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:05,440
So if you get into trouble, that doesn't mean you're a troublemaker. It means you need to try harder to act, right?

127
00:13:06,918 --> 00:13:12,320
If you get a bad grade ve, that doesn't mean you're stupid, it just means you need to spend more time studying.

128
00:13:13,918 --> 00:13:20,820
No one's born being good at all things. You become good at things through hard work.

129
00:13:21,678 --> 00:13:29,298
You're not a varsity athlete. The first time you play a new sport, you don't hit every note. The first time you sing a song, you've got to practice.

130
00:13:29,538 --> 00:13:32,639
The same principle applies to your schoolwork.

131
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:45,538
You might have to do a math problem a few times before you get it right. You might have to read something a few times before you understand it. You definitely have to do a few drafts of a paper before it's good enough to hand in.

132
00:13:47,139 --> 00:13:49,019
Don't be afraid to ask questions.

133
00:13:49,298 --> 00:13:53,259
Don't be afraid to ask for help when you need it. I do that every day.

134
00:13:54,158 --> 00:13:57,480
Asking for help isn't a sign of weakness, it's a sign of strength.

135
00:13:57,720 --> 00:14:04,200
Because it shows you have the courage to admit when you don't know something, and that then allows you to learn something new.

136
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:14,940
So find an adult that you trust, a parent, a grandparent or a teacher, a coach or a counselor, and ask them to help you stay on track to meet your goals.

137
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:19,519
And even when you're struggling, even when you're discouraged.

138
00:14:19,759 --> 00:14:22,980
And you feel like other people have given up on you.

139
00:14:23,379 --> 00:14:25,759
Don't ever give up on yourself.

140
00:14:26,678 --> 00:14:28,840
Because when you give up on yourself.

141
00:14:29,080 --> 00:14:30,480
You give up on your country.

142
00:14:31,580 --> 00:14:35,480
The story of America isn't about people who quit when things got tough.

143
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:41,740
It's about people who kept going, who tried harder, who loved their country too much to do anything less than their best.

144
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:51,860
It's the story of students who sat, where you sit 250 years ago and went on to wage a revolution. And they founded this nation, Young people.

145
00:14:52,918 --> 00:14:58,860
Students who sat where you sit 75 years ago, who overcame a depression and won a World War.

146
00:14:59,100 --> 00:15:02,158
Who fought for civil rights and put a man on the moon.

147
00:15:02,658 --> 00:15:10,240
Students who sat where you sit 20 years ago, who founded Google and Twitter and Facebook and changed the way we communicate with each other.

148
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:14,440
So today, I want to ask all of you.

149
00:15:15,038 --> 00:15:17,100
What's your contribution going to be?

150
00:15:17,759 --> 00:15:20,178
What problems are you going to solve?

151
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:23,120
What discoveries will you make?

152
00:15:24,580 --> 00:15:30,500
What will a president who comes here in 20 or 50 or 100 years say about what all of you did for this country?

153
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:39,620
Now your families, your teachers and I are doing everything we can to make sure you have the education you need to answer these questions.

154
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:45,418
I'm working hard to fix up your classrooms and get you the books and the equipment and the computers you need to learn.

155
00:15:46,678 --> 00:15:48,480
But you've got to do your part too.

156
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:53,440
So I expect all of you to get serious this year.

157
00:15:54,340 --> 00:15:57,960
Expect you to put your best effort into everything you do.

158
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:01,879
I expect great things from each of you.

159
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:04,700
So don't let us down.

160
00:16:05,340 --> 00:16:10,158
Don't let your family down or your country down. Most of all, don't let yourself down.

161
00:16:11,798 --> 00:16:13,158
Make us all proud.

162
00:16:13,820 --> 00:16:17,259
Thank you very much, everybody. God bless you. God bless America.

163
00:16:18,820 --> 00:16:19,700
Thank you.
